WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:06.999
A recorded run on a synthetic case: the patient, the laboratory and all nine reports are fictional.

2
00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:15.456
On the latest report, collected 8 September 2026, potassium is printed as 6.4 mmol/L.

3
00:00:15.606 --> 00:00:18.489
Printed interval: 3.5 to 5.1.

4
00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:26.159
On the five earlier collection dates, from March 2024, it was 4.4 to 4.8, inside the interval.

5
00:00:26.309 --> 00:00:27.861
Then 6.4, above it.

6
00:00:28.867 --> 00:00:33.540
A test driver uploaded nine synthetic reports, 30 pages, out of date order.

7
00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:37.792
Each value is placed by the date printed on its report, not by upload order.

8
00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:41.005
The first report asks whose report it is.

9
00:00:41.155 --> 00:00:45.248
The run confirmed the printed patient ID, and the other eight reports matched it.

10
00:00:46.267 --> 00:00:52.128
Rebuilt in a new browser from the server, the record lists 103 stored values from 9 reports,

11
00:00:52.278 --> 00:00:54.970
each with its date, printed interval and file.

12
00:00:55.120 --> 00:01:03.837
Creatinine printed as 80 µmol/L, a different unit, was kept as printed and out of the mg/dL series, with the reason shown.

13
00:01:04.867 --> 00:01:13.253
History draws one potassium series across six collection dates, from 4.4 in March 2024 to 6.4 in September 2026.

14
00:01:14.267 --> 00:01:19.066
The first reading leads with Critical Potassium (6.4 mmol/L), at urgency Emergency.

15
00:01:19.216 --> 00:01:20.852
Evidence support: Moderate.

16
00:01:21.867 --> 00:01:25.644
It states its coverage: 13 of 19 value-bearing lines read.

17
00:01:25.794 --> 00:01:27.732
"Not evaluated does not mean normal."

18
00:01:27.882 --> 00:01:34.714
Two rows were not stored, and are named: a second creatinine, 1.62, and an unreadable white cell count.

19
00:01:35.733 --> 00:01:41.392
Creatinine is printed twice, so 1.04 is held for review and neither value is chosen.

20
00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:49.574
It issued three questions: on potassium 6.4, on HbA1c 6.8, and on family history.

21
00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:56.521
The answers are scripted: No, No and Yes, set before the run and clicked by a synthetic evaluator account.

22
00:01:57.533 --> 00:02:02.121
The two No answers were recorded and changed nothing; no rule in this version uses them.

23
00:02:02.271 --> 00:02:08.259
The Yes changed familial dyslipidaemia from "sporadic assumed" to "inherited/atherogenic risk raised".

24
00:02:08.409 --> 00:02:11.209
Confidence and urgency are stated as unchanged.

25
00:02:12.233 --> 00:02:16.374
After the answers, the finding is still Critical Potassium, at Emergency.

26
00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:21.564
In the new browser, all nine original reports reopened as whole PDFs.

27
00:02:21.714 --> 00:02:24.726
Linking each value to its place on the page is being validated.

28
00:02:25.733 --> 00:02:31.402
Both readings were exported as review snapshots, primary concern Critical Potassium, as on screen.

29
00:02:31.552 --> 00:02:36.731
Because the creatinine conflict is unresolved, each export is marked not for clinical use.

30
00:02:36.881 --> 00:02:40.530
A new browser downloaded the recorded snapshots again, unchanged.

31
00:02:41.533 --> 00:02:47.374
This was a recorded run of LabSense, a Clinical Investigational System in active development, on a synthetic case.

32
00:02:47.524 --> 00:02:50.372
Further features are being validated and are not shown here.

33
00:02:50.522 --> 00:02:52.500
No AI model was called in this run.

34
00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:54.808
LabSense has not been clinically validated.

